  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different aquatic and coastal systems can be pushed across ecological and geomorphic “tipping points” by climate change or land-use change. It links changing drivers—such as longer wet or dry periods, stronger flood extremes, sea-level rise, altered sediment supply, and channel narrowing or deepening—to abrupt shifts in system state.\n\nAcross rivers, estuaries, deltas, and tidal channels, the central message is that gradual external pressures can trigger self-reinforcing transitions in flow, sediment transport, and habitat structure. In some cases, increased discharge causes erosion, soil saturation, and eventually a shift toward more turbid, flood-dominated conditions with a smoother, deeper bed and fewer channel distinctions. Elsewhere, sediment retention or pulses interact with altered hydraulics to deplete pools, homogenize channels, or change flood timing. In lowland and coastal settings, flow obstruction, subsidence, and sea-level rise increase inundation stress, causing drowning, loss of channel networks, or simpler floodplain patterns. Another pathway shows channel infilling and elevation gain, where sheltered conditions and storm-delivered sediment help new landforms establish and vegetation appears to grow faster over time.\n\nOverall, the diagram presents multiple pathways by which hydro-geomorphic landscapes reorganize into simpler, more turbid, drowned, or newly established states.",
